    "networkHelpTitle": "How the network works",
    "networkHelpAlgorithmTitle": "How the algorithm works",
    "networkHelpAlgorithmText": "The network is built from valid articles: each author (Co-authorship tab) or keyword (Keywords tab) becomes a node. Two nodes are connected by an edge when they appear together in at least one article. The Louvain algorithm groups nodes into communities (clusters), which are assigned different colors. The layout is generated by a force-directed simulation: connected nodes attract each other, disconnected nodes repel each other, and collision forces prevent overlaps.",
    "networkHelpNodeTitle": "Node size",
    "networkHelpNodeText": "The size of the node is proportional to the frequency of occurrence: the number of articles in which the keyword appears (Keywords tab) or the number of the author's publications (Co-authorship tab). The higher the frequency, the larger the node and the label.",
    "networkHelpEdgeTitle": "Edges (links between nodes)",
    "networkHelpEdgeText": "An edge connects two nodes that appear together in at least one article. The thickness is proportional to the weight of the relationship: in the full counting method, the weight is the number of articles in which the pair appears together; in the fractional counting method, it is the sum of the fractional weights calculated for each article.",
    "networkHelpCountingTitle": "Fractional × full counting",
    "networkHelpCountingText": "In the full counting method, each pair of nodes from the same article is assigned a weight of 1. Consequently, articles with many authors or keywords can inflate the number of connections. In the fractional counting method, each article contributes a total weight of 1, divided equally among all pairs (1 divided by the number of pairs), thereby avoiding this bias. The fractional method is the recommended one (Perianes-Rodriguez, Waltman & van Eck, 2016)."
  }
